  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:Y_zyGtmETWQ",
    "purpose": "Make French toast while testing whether stale, oven-dried or fresh Japanese milk bread (shokupan) makes the best French toast, explaining the science of staling versus drying along the way.",
    "video_type": "comparison",
    "thesis": "Staling (starch retrogradation) and drying are different things. With a soft but well-structured bread like shokupan, fresh bread can work for French toast. Which bread to use, and how to prepare it, depends on the type of loaf.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Staling is starch retrogradation (recrystallization); drying removes moisture without retrograding the starch, so dried bread stays tender like a cracker.",
      "Three slices of shokupan were tested: left out uncovered overnight (stale), dried in a 225°F oven for about 20 minutes, and fresh.",
      "The custard is a hybrid of Yvonne Ruperti's and Daniel Gritzer's recipes: a cup of buttermilk, three eggs, brown sugar, vanilla, poudre douce spice and melted butter.",
      "White sugar is sprinkled on the top before flipping, which forms a caramelized crème brûlée-like crust (a Daniel Gritzer technique).",
      "He doesn't clarify the butter for French toast because it isn't cooked very hot and some brown butter flavor is welcome.",
      "American supermarket sandwich bread must be staled or dried, or it turns soupy. Shokupan can be used fresh. Hearty sourdough should stay fairly fresh because dry loaves absorb custard poorly and stay tough.",
      "In the tasting, the stale and dried slices were very similar. The fresh shokupan was the most custardy, and he preferred it.",
      "For a crowd, use a flat-top griddle or hold batches on a rack in a low oven. Lan Lam's sheet-tray oven method is another option."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to toast",
        "summary": "A cold-open joke about pain perdu, then Kenji starts butter in the pan and explains why he doesn't clarify butter for French toast.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "The science of stale bread",
        "summary": "He introduces the stale, oven-dried and fresh shokupan slices and explains how staling (starch retrogradation) differs from drying. He also credits the friends whose recipes his is based on.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Making the custard batter",
        "summary": "He builds the custard and compares milk options. He then covers the sugar, spices and eggs, and notes what French toast is called in different countries.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking the french toast",
        "summary": "The slices are soaked and fried, sugar is sprinkled on top before flipping, and he gives tips for cooking for a crowd and holding batches in a low oven.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Cooking techniques and history",
        "summary": "Covers the caramelized sugar crust, the origin of the name pain perdu, how much butter actually ends up in the food, and the vintage cast iron pan. It ends with Lan Lam's sheet-tray oven method for a crowd.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 15
      },
      {
        "title": "Bread type comparisons",
        "summary": "Drawing on Serious Eats testing, he explains how supermarket sandwich bread, shokupan and sourdough each behave and whether each should be fresh, stale or dried.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "Taste testing results",
        "summary": "He cuts and tastes the three slices. Stale and dried are comparable, and fresh is the most custardy. He finishes by tasting all three stacked with maple syrup.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 19
      },
      {
        "title": "Conclusion and serving",
        "summary": "He recaps the experiment and his preference for fresh shokupan, points to the written recipe and his friends' recipes, feeds the dog and signs off.",
        "first_segment": 19,
        "last_segment": 20
      }
    ],
